{"name":"Teal Beak","hex":"#006767","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"teal","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of various small freshwater ducks of the genus Anas that are brightly coloured and have short necks.","A dark, somewhat bluish-green colour; a dark cyan."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having a bluish-green colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/teal"},{"term":"beak","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A rigid structure projecting from the front of a bird's face, used for pecking, grooming, foraging, carrying items, eating food, etc.","A rigid structure projecting from the front of a bird's face, used for pecking, grooming, foraging, carrying items, eating food, etc."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To strike with the beak.","To seize with the beak."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/beak"}],"entries":[{"title":"Beak","blocks":[{"text":"The beak or bill is an external rostrum structure found mostly in birds. A beak is used for pecking, grasping, and holding, preening, courtship, and feeding young. The terms beak and rostrum are also used to refer to a similar organ in some non-avian theropods, ornithischians, pterosaurs, cetaceans, dicynodonts, rhynchosaurs, anuran tadpoles, turtles, monotremes, sirens, pufferfish, billfishes, parrotfish and cephalopods."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Beak"},{"title":"Teal","blocks":[{"text":"Teal is a dark cyan color. Its name comes from that of a bird, the Eurasian teal which has a similarly colored stripe on its head. The word is often used colloquially to refer to shades of cyan in general."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1a/Smithsonian_-_NMAAHC_-_Teal_blue_dress_and_cropped_jacket_designed_by_Ann_Lowe-_NMAAHC_2014.73.2.1-.2.jpg/330px-Smithsonian_-_NMAAHC_-_Teal_blue_dress_and_cropped_jacket_designed_by_Ann_Lowe-_NMAAHC_2014.73.2.1-.2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&ut